WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.260
Every shift in a SOC begins and ends at the same place: the SIEM.

2
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:10.490
When an alert lands in your queue, it didn't appear by magic. Let's follow its whole journey.

3
00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:18.020
A SIEM — Security Information and Event Management — is the single pane of glass for the whole environment.

4
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:26.950
Logs from firewalls, endpoints, identity providers, and cloud all converge here, get normalized, and get evaluated against detection logic.

5
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:32.780
Every log takes the same journey into the SIEM. First it's collected from the source.

6
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:40.460
Then it's parsed — broken into fields — and normalized, so a username from a firewall and from a cloud log share one field name.

7
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.160
It's stored so it can be queried, and made searchable across millions of events.

8
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:52.050
Collect, parse, normalize, store, search — that pipeline is what makes detection possible at all.

9
00:00:52.550 --> 00:01:00.570
Detection rules turn stored events into alerts in four main ways. A signature matches a known-bad pattern.

10
00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:06.210
A threshold fires when a count crosses a line — ten failed logins in a minute.

11
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:13.670
Correlation ties events across different sources into one story — a login, then a download, then an upload.

12
00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:21.350
And anomaly detection flags a deviation from the baseline — something simply unusual for this host or user.

13
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:26.990
Here's the mindset shift. An alert is not a verdict handed down from an oracle.

14
00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:35.340
It's a question the SIEM is asking you — this pattern matched; is it real? Your job is to answer it, not just to close it.

15
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:43.090
Understand the full journey — raw log, pipeline, rule, alert — and you triage faster.

16
00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:49.640
You stop treating alerts as truth, and start treating them as the starting point of an investigation.
